75: Sort Colors
 Today I solved the Sort Colors problem using the Quick Sort algorithm. The array contains only three values: 0, 1, and 2, where each value represents a color. The goal is to arrange all the 0s first, followed by 1s, and then 2s without using the built-in sorting function.

For this solution, I implemented Quick Sort manually. I selected the first element as the pivot and used a partitioning technique to place the pivot in its correct position. After partitioning, I recursively sorted the left and right parts of the array until the entire array became sorted.


Example 1:

Input: nums = [2,0,2,1,1,0]
Output: [0,0,1,1,2,2]

<-----------------------My code-------------------------->
  class Solution {
public:
    int divide(vector<int>& a,int low,int high){
        int pivot=a[low];
        int i=low,j=high;
        while(i<j){
            while( i<=j && a[i]<=pivot ){
                i++;
            }
            while( j>=i &&a[j]>pivot  ){
                j--;
            }
            if(i<j){
                swap(a[i],a[j]);
            }
          

        }
          swap(a[j],a[low]);
          return j;
    }

    void quicksort(vector<int>& v,int low,int high){
        if(low<high){
            int p=divide(v,low,high);
            quicksort(v,low,p-1);
            quicksort(v,p+1,high);
        }
    }

    void sortColors(vector<int>& nums) {
        int high=nums.size()-1;
        quicksort(nums,0,high);

    }
};

